WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.260
I want you to name the three worst greenhouse gases, carbon dioxide, methane, and then we just kind of sit here and watch

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.060
as the

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.740
third one does not arrive.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.520
Right. Yeah, it rarely does. People, they almost always draw blank on that third one, which is nitrous oxide.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:32.080
Which is exactly why we are dedicating this deep dive to it. Today, we're digging into a student project by Saanvi for the 3M Young Scientist Challenge 2027. We are looking at how hacking a microscopic ecosystem might just save us from this totally invisible climate threat.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:42.820
And the stakes? I mean, they really could not be higher here. One single molecule of nitrous oxide traps 273 times more heat than carbon dioxide.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.040
But 273 times?

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:51.220
Yeah, it's wild. It is also the absolute worst thing still eating away at the ozone layer. Wow.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:01:06.640
Okay, so the mechanics of how it actually gets up there are, they're surprisingly simple. Like picture a farm, a harvested crop leaves fertilizer behind in the dirt. Right. Soil bacteria eat what is left and out comes nitrous oxide just drifting up

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:21.420
like invisible smoke. Yeah. And unless something physically takes it apart, it just escapes. But the catch is only one specific set of genes can do that. Just one? Just one set of genes, yeah. And the vast majority of soil bacteria simply do not have it.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:29.220
Which, you know, sounds like a complete dead end. I mean, we cannot choose which microbes live in a giant field of dirt. So micromanaging an entire acre just feels

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:39.740
completely impossible. It usually is impossible. But there is, there's this one single corner of farming where we actually do pick the microbes. And that is soybeans.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.100
Oh, right. Because for a soybean, the bacteria are the fertilizer.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:50.800
Exactly. So every spring, farmers take the seed and they physically coat it with live bacteria before planting.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:02:00.060
But like putting bacteria on a seed does not guarantee they survive. Once that seed is in the ground, this intense turf war begins. They are all racing for the

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:05.260
nodules. Right. And just to define that, nodules are the places on a root where the bacteria live.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.180
Like tiny underground houses.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:16.200
Yes, exactly like tiny underground houses. But the surrounding soil is already incredibly crowded with native bacteria. And they're all fighting to get inside.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.620
Oh, so if you are a latecomer, you are locked out entirely.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:27.660
Completely locked out. Those native soil bacteria have a massive home field advantage. They know the dirt. They are incredibly fast.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.400
Which perfectly explains why scientists were stuck for 13 years. Like they kept trying to fix

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.900
this from the wrong side. Yet they tried putting that specific set of genes into a newcomer strain of bacteria.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:49.680
But the newcomer always loses the race to the bacteria already living in the soil. It's like trying to give a VIP pass to someone who doesn't even know where the club is while the locals just walk right through the door.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.020
That is exactly it.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.660
And if the wrong bacteria win...

00:02:53.660 --> 00:03:04.740
It gets worse, right. Oh yeah, if you swap a strain that destroys nitrous oxide for one that cannot, those same soybeans release 18 times more nitrous oxide. 18

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:05.680
times more.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:17.120
That is a disaster. A huge disaster. So to break that cycle, you need this brilliant inversion from the project. The idea was, you know, do not make the good strain

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.580
competitive. Make the competitive strain good.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:25.880
Yes. Hand the set of genes to the bacteria farmers put on the seed. The ones that already win the race.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:31.900
You just hand the VIP pass directly to the bouncer. That engineered solution is named NitRobe.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:38.540
Right. NitRobe. Which brings up the real test. Does this actually work out there in the dirt? Well,

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:46.180
we know part of the puzzle. Like a machine learning model already found 10 previously unknown species that destroy nitrous oxide on their own. Yeah,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:55.460
but the next episode of this deep dive will cover the lab experiments. Like, does the bioengineered strain really destroy nitrous oxide? And does it still win the nodules?

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:02.540
We will definitely get into all that. And just so you know, all of this is entirely written up at the NitRobe website, which is where the detail lives.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:10.640
It really leaves you wondering what other massive planetary crises we could solve just by hacking the organisms that already dominate an ecosystem.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.840
You cannot engineer a winner, so you inherit one.
